 ## Overview
-Interpolation and Sampling Techniques constitute the family of methods responsible for retrieving scalar values from discrete voxel grids at arbitrary query positions along viewing rays, thereby reconstructing a continuous scalar field to mitigate discretization artifacts such as aliasing and blockiness [S1, unofficial][S2]. This family solves the fundamental problem of spatial reconstruction in direct volume ray casting, distinguishing itself from sibling techniques like [Multi-Sample Anti-Aliasing via Ray Distribution](1219-multi-sample-anti-aliasing-via-ray-distribution.md) by focusing on the spatial reconstruction kernel itself rather than the statistical distribution of sample points, and from [Depth to Distance Conversion](2428-depth-to-distance-conversion.md) by operating in voxel space rather than depth space.
+Interpolation and Sampling Techniques define how continuous scalar fields are reconstructed from discrete voxel data to evaluate density or opacity at arbitrary query positions along viewing rays [10526-interpolation-methods.md]. This family solves the problem of discretization artifacts (aliasing, blockiness) inherent in nearest-neighbor sampling while balancing computational cost against spatial smoothness [S2, unofficial][S3]. It sets itself apart from sibling techniques like [Supersampling](3493-supersampling.md) (which increases sample count) or [Multi-Sample Anti-Aliasing via Ray Distribution](1219-multi-sample-anti-aliasing-via-ray-distribution.md) (which distributes rays) by focusing on the mathematical reconstruction of the field value itself from existing samples [10526-interpolation-methods.md].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All interpolation schemes in this family map a query position $\mathbf{p}$ in voxel space to a scalar value $V(\mathbf{p})$ by combining a finite set of neighboring voxel values $V_i$ using a set of weights $w_i(\mathbf{p})$:
-$$ V(\mathbf{p}) = \sum_{i} w_i(\mathbf{p}) V_i $$
-The weights are derived from a reconstruction kernel $h(\mathbf{z})$, where $\mathbf{z}$ is the distance from the query point to the sample center [G]. The choice of kernel defines the trade-off between computational complexity, spatial extent (number of neighbors), and frequency response (aliasing vs. blurring) [S1, unofficial][S2].
