 ### [Depth to Distance Conversion](2428-depth-to-distance-conversion.md)
-Depth to Distance Conversion transforms sampled depth values ($z_{vals}$) along a ray into segment lengths ($dists$) required for volume rendering integration. It accounts for the viewing angle by scaling the depth differences with the cosine of the angle between the ray direction and the viewing direction. This method is distinct from [Interpolation Schemes for Volume Rendering](817-interpolation-schemes-for-volume-rendering.md) as it focuses on metric length calculation rather than value estimation, and from [Sampling and Jittering](2516-sampling-and-jittering.md) as it operates on already sampled points [G].
+Depth to Distance Conversion transforms discrete depth samples ($z\_vals$) along a ray into physical segment lengths ($dists$) required for volume rendering integration. It solves the problem that raw depth differences do not represent true Euclidean distances when the ray is not aligned with the viewing direction. It differs from [NDC Points to Distance](2500-ndc-points-to-distance.md) by operating in camera-space depth coordinates rather than Normalized Device Coordinates, making it suitable for standard perspective projection pipelines where depth is linear in camera space but non-linear in screen space.
 
 ### [Alpha Grid Mask](2430-alpha-grid-mask.md)
-The Alpha Grid Mask is a module that stores a 3D alpha volume and provides efficient sampling of alpha values at arbitrary 3D coordinates using PyTorch's `grid_sample` function, enabling ray filtering based on opacity. It serves as the core sampling primitive for volume rendering to incorporate spatially varying opacity constraints derived from external supervision or geometry masks. It differs from direct intensity sampling by applying a linear remapping to the sampled values to ensure they fall within a valid $[min\_opacity, max\_opacity]$ interval, preventing complete occlusion or invisibility artifacts.
